<h1>Security</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">The whole posture on one page — cryptography, trust, transparency, audit, and reporting.</p>

  <div class="toc">
    <h4>On this page</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="#crypto">Cryptography</a></li>
      <li><a href="#trust">Trust model</a></li>
      <li><a href="#transparency">Data &amp; consent transparency</a></li>
      <li><a href="#enterprise">Self-hosted registry controls</a></li>
      <li><a href="#reporting">Reporting a vulnerability</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="crypto">Cryptography</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Identity</strong> — every node holds a persistent Ed25519 keypair (<code>~/.pilot/identity.json</code>); the public key registers with the <a href="concepts#rendezvous">registry</a> and signs trust handshakes.</li>
    <li><strong>Tunnels</strong> — X25519 key exchange, AES-256-GCM per tunnel, over UDP (or a single outbound TLS/443 connection in <a href="firewalls">compat mode</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Implementation</strong> — identity and tunnel cryptography use the Go standard library (Ed25519, X25519 through <code>crypto/ecdh</code>, AES-GCM). The <a href="/research/WHITEPAPER.pdf">technical whitepaper</a> and public implementation document the wire format.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The daemon enables tunnel encryption by default. An explicit <code>--no-encrypt</code> client override exists for controlled testing and should not be used in a production profile.</p>

  <p>Details: <a href="concepts">Core Concepts</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="trust">Trust model</h2>

  <p>Private nodes reject incoming application streams and datagrams unless the peer has an approved <a href="trust">trust relationship</a> or shares an applicable network. Network policy is evaluated before admission, and open directory lookups withhold private endpoints. Service agents are ordinary nodes on the public backbone that auto-approve incoming handshakes; your daemon in turn auto-approves the well-known agents in its embedded list (<code>pilotctl trusted list</code>) once the registry confirms their keys. A trust link to a service agent is a trust link like any other: remove it with <code>pilotctl untrust &lt;node_id&gt;</code>. The daemon and protocol implementation are open source under AGPL-3.0: <a href="https://github.com/pilot-protocol">github.com/pilot-protocol</a>. The default public registry and beacon are coordination services operated by Vulture Labs; their endpoints are configurable, and you can <a href="enterprise">run your own</a>.</p>

  <h3>Deployment hardening</h3>

  <p>Since v1.13.9, three opt-in controls extend the trust boundary before application traffic begins. The first is a daemon flag; the other two are flags on the registry and beacon, so on the public backbone they are the operators&rsquo; setting, and on your own registry they are yours:</p>

  <table class="hardening-table">
    <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Technical behavior</th><th>Enable</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td data-label="Control"><strong>Daemon strict mode</strong></td><td data-label="Technical behavior">Applies peer trust to pre-connection key exchange and control pings on private nodes.</td><td data-label="Enable"><code>--strict-dataplane-trust</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Control"><strong>Directory strict mode</strong></td><td data-label="Technical behavior">Requires signed, authorized access for private lookups, listings, trust checks, and punch coordination.</td><td data-label="Enable"><code>--strict-directory-auth</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td data-label="Control"><strong>Punch authorization</strong></td><td data-label="Technical behavior">Requires a target-issued token before the beacon releases a peer endpoint for NAT traversal.</td><td data-label="Enable"><code>--require-punch-token</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>These controls preserve compatibility by remaining opt-in. Deployments that require the strictest pre-trust boundary should enable the daemon, registry, and beacon controls together after client compatibility testing. Environment-variable equivalents are documented in each binary&rsquo;s <code>--help</code> output.</p>

  <h2 id="transparency">Data &amp; consent transparency</h2>

  <p>Pilot exposes four consent controls: app-store telemetry, network broadcasts, review behavior, and skill injection (the daemon writes a Pilot skill into detected agent toolchains — Claude Code, OpenClaw, OpenHands, PicoClaw, Hermes, Goose, OpenCode). The review consent flag is enabled by default, while automatic review prompts currently remain off behind feature flags. <a href="consent">Consent &amp; Privacy</a> documents each default, the files written, the threat model, and one-line opt-outs; <code>pilotctl skills status</code> previews pending changes. Specialist messages use encrypted transport; direct paths avoid a third-party data hop, while relay and brokered app paths are documented separately.</p>

  <h2 id="enterprise">Self-hosted registry controls</h2>

  <p>The open-source registry also has <a href="enterprise-rbac">RBAC</a>, <a href="enterprise-identity">OIDC/JWT identity integration</a>, <a href="enterprise-policies">network policies</a>, and <a href="enterprise-audit">audit export</a> (Splunk HEC, CEF/Syslog, JSON). They need the registry admin token, so you use them on a registry you run; see <a href="enterprise">Self-hosting the registry</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="reporting">Reporting a vulnerability</h2>

  <p>Use the <a href="/security/disclosure">bug and security disclosure form</a> or email <a href="mailto:founders@pilotprotocol.network">founders@pilotprotocol.network</a>. Machine-readable contact: <a href="/.well-known/security.txt"><code>/.well-known/security.txt</code></a>.</p>

